---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Exporting"
  description="Save a still frame or record your composition to video."
>
  <h2>Save image</h2>
  <p>
    Click <strong>Save image</strong> in the sidebar to export the current
    stage frame as a PNG. The image uses the canvas resolution configured in
    <a href="/docs/projects">Project settings</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Save video</h2>
  <div class="placeholder">Screenshot: Save Video dialog</div>
  <p>
    Click <strong>Save video</strong> to open the export dialog. Astrofox
    records the stage in real time.
  </p>

  <h3>Audio source</h3>
  <p>
    Pick the audio file to encode into the video. This can be the same file you
    loaded in the player or a different one (handy if you want a clean source
    without effects). Required.
  </p>

  <h3>Save location</h3>
  <p>Choose where the <code>.webm</code> file goes. Required.</p>

  <h3>Time duration</h3>
  <p>
    Set the start and end time. The minimum export length is 5 seconds. The
    range is shown both as time inputs and as a draggable handle over the
    waveform.
  </p>

  <h3>Include audio</h3>
  <p>
    On by default &mdash; the audio track is encoded into the video. Turn it
    off to get a silent video file.
  </p>

  <h3>Recording</h3>
  <p>
    Once started, Astrofox plays the track from the chosen start time and
    records the stage frame-by-frame. Keep the tab focused while recording.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    Output is WebM (VP9). You can convert to MP4 with a tool like FFmpeg if
    you need a different container.
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
